# Snyk Code findings that have been read and accepted, with the reason for each.
#
# Why this file exists instead of the ignore: block in .snyk
#
# `snyk code test` does not apply .snyk ignore entries. Checked against
# SnykCode 1.1307.3 on 2026-09-18: with nine paths listed under ignore:, the
# SARIF it produced carried zero suppressions and the command still exited 1.
# Dismissing an alert in the GitHub Security tab does not help either; that
# changes what GitHub displays and has no effect on the exit code of the scan
# that produced it. The exclude: block in .snyk does work, so files that are
# not application code are still handled there.
#
# So the check had two possible states: red forever, or a threshold raised
# until it stopped asking. A red check that is red for reasons nobody has
# looked at in months tells nobody anything, and that is what it had become.
#
# This file is the third option. The scan still runs at --severity-threshold=low,
# so Snyk still reports everything it finds. Its findings are then compared
# against this list. Anything not on the list fails the build, and so does a
# second finding of a rule in a file where one was accepted. The reasons stay
# in the repository, where a reviewer reads them in the diff and a contributor
# can check them against the code.
#
# Format, one accepted finding group per line:
#
#     rule | path | count | reason
#
# count is how many findings of that rule in that file have been accepted. Line
# numbers are deliberately absent: they move whenever the file above them
# changes, and a gate that fails on a refactor gets switched off.
#
# Adding a line is a decision that the finding is not a vulnerability, or is
# one that cannot be fixed here. It is not a way to get a green check. If the
# reason cannot be written down, the finding is not accepted.

# --- Rules that read a protocol's choice of algorithm as this code's choice ---

php/InsecureHash | cms/app/lib/plPasswordBreach.php | 1 | The Have I Been Pwned range API is defined in terms of SHA-1: the client sends the first five characters of the SHA-1 of the password and matches the returned suffixes. SHA-1 is the wire format, not a choice, and the digest is never stored. Sending anything else would simply return no results.
php/InsecureHash | cms/services/twilio.php | 1 | Twilio signs its webhooks with HMAC-SHA1 and sends the result in X-Twilio-Signature. Verifying the signature means computing the same HMAC-SHA1; any other algorithm would reject every genuine request. The comparison already uses hash_equals().
php/InsecureHash | cms/app/lib/pikaCrypto.php | 1 | TOTP is RFC 6238, whose default and near-universal algorithm is HMAC-SHA1. Authenticator apps generate codes that way, so the server has to check them that way. The secret is 20 bytes from random_bytes().
php/InsecureHash | cms/ops/transfer_case.php | 1 | The v5 transfer receiver rejects a packet with no "checksum" field, so the field stays for interoperability with deployments this code cannot upgrade. It is not the authenticity check: that is HTTP Basic plus the HMAC signature build_transfer_packet() adds.

# --- Stored md5 digests that have to stay readable until the last row migrates ---

php/InsecureHash | cms/app/lib/pikaAuthDb.php | 2 | Legacy password migration. Rows created before password_hash() hold an md5 digest, so sign-in has to try md5 to let those users in at all. The match is hash_equals(), not ==, and a successful legacy match immediately rewrites the row with password_hash() and audits it as legacy_md5_upgraded, so each account passes through this path once and never again.
php/InsecureHash | cms/app/lib/pl.php | 1 | Same legacy digest as pikaAuthDb.php, read here during the password-plus-TOTP reauthentication check. password_verify() is tried first and md5 only as the fallback, through hash_equals().
php/InsecureHash | cms/password.php | 2 | The change-password form has to recognise the current password to accept a change, and for a not-yet-migrated account that means comparing the md5 digest on the row. Both comparisons use hash_equals(); the replacement password is written with password_hash().
php/InsecureHash | cms/dataops.php | 1 | The same current-password check as password.php, on the form-post path. hash_equals() against the stored digest, password_verify() first.

